    <div class="container">
      <h1>Cold Cranking Amps (CCA) — Reference</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Data · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> CCA is a cold-start power rating measured under a specific standard. A value
        without its standard is not comparable — a JIS number (tested at −15°C) reads higher than an EN/SAE number
        (tested at −18°C) for the same physical battery. See the
        <a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">main-site article</a> for the full explanation.
      </div>

      <h2>Standards at a glance</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Standard</th><th>Region</th><th>Temperature</th><th>Cut-off / method</th><th>Effect on number</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {standards.map((s) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{s.std}</strong></td><td>{s.region}</td><td>{s.temp}</td><td>{s.cutoff}</td><td>{s.effect}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Typical CCA by battery group</h2>
      <p>Typical cold cranking amps and capacity by group. Values are cross-verified ranges (±10–15% within a group),
      not a substitute for a specific battery's datasheet.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Typical capacity</th><th>Typical CCA</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {groups.map((x) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{x.g}</strong></td><td>{x.ah}</td><td>{x.cca}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The temperature ladder</h2>
      <p>
        CCA, CA, MCA and HCA are the same 30-second test at different temperatures — warmer tests print higher
        numbers. The ladder, from coldest to warmest, is:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>CCA</strong> — −18°C (0°F); the number to trust for cold starts.</li>
        <li><strong>CA / MCA</strong> — 0°C (32°F); ~20–25% higher than CCA for the same battery.</li>
        <li><strong>HCA</strong> — 27°C (80°F); the highest, rarely the number to buy against.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Key relationships</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>CCA vs Ah</strong> — CCA is instantaneous cranking power (short, high-current burst); Ah is total stored energy (long, low-current discharge). Independent dimensions — no fixed multiplier.</li>
        <li><strong>CCA vs internal resistance</strong> — CCA and internal resistance are inversely related: lower resistance allows higher cranking current, which is why conductance testers estimate CCA from resistance.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to compare two CCA numbers</h2>
      <p>
        Compare <strong>within one standard</strong>: JIS to JIS, EN to EN. To compare across the temperature ladder,
        convert MCA to CCA by dividing by roughly 1.2–1.25. Always ask "which standard, and at what temperature" —
        that one question resolves most specification confusion.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>SAE J537 — cranking-ampere method (CCA −18°C, CA 0°C, HCA 27°C).</li>
        <li>EN 50342 / JIS D5301 / DIN 43539 — European, Japanese and German test methods.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer datasheets (Varta, Bosch, Yuasa, Century, Exide) — typical CCA by group.</li>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — CCA definition and group-size reference.</li>
      </ul>

